# Authoring on Peerfold

Apply these whenever you create or change course content through the Peerfold MCP
server or the REST API. They are the four contracts an integration gets wrong.

## Never guess a block shape

A lesson body is an ordered array of blocks. A block is `{ type, props }`, plus
`children` on the container types. Ask for the shape instead of inventing it:

1. `list_block_types` — every type the registry allows, with its label, category,
   whether it nests children, and its prop names.
2. `get_block_schema` — one type's JSON Schema, its default props, and a
   pasteable example node.
3. `validate_blocks` — a free dry run that returns the editor's exact per-block
   errors. Keep the `normalized` array it hands back so block ids stay stable.
4. `update_lesson_blocks` — the write.

The same registry drives the editor, the renderer, SCORM export and these tools,
so content written this way renders identically to content typed by hand. Clients
that surface MCP resources can read the same reference at
`peerfold://block-schema-reference.json`.

## Read before every whole-document write

Several tools replace an entire document rather than patching it. Read the
current state first or you delete what you did not resend:

| Replaces the whole thing | Read it with |
| --- | --- |
| `update_lesson_blocks` | `get_lesson_blocks` |
| `set_exam_questions` | `get_exam` |
| `set_access_rules` | `get_course` |
| `set_course_ceus` | `get_course_ceus` |
| `set_homepage` | `get_homepage` (+ `get_homepage_schema`) |
| `set_portal_menu` | `get_portal_menu` |
| `set_portal_footer` | `get_portal_footer` |
| `set_course_landing` / `set_program_landing` (the `sections` document) | `get_course` / `get_program`, plus `get_program_landing_schema` |

`set_course_settings` is the exception: it is a merge patch field by field. Send a
value to change it, omit it to keep it, send `null` to clear it. `set_course_landing`
is honestly split — its `enabled` flag merges, its `sections` document replaces,
and each argument is optional, so neither can silently undo the other.

Every tool's arguments are a strict object. A misspelled or unsupported key is
refused by name rather than dropped, so fix the name instead of retrying with
more fields.

## Exam rules and exam questions are two different calls

`set_course_settings { exam }` carries the RULES — passing score, attempts,
whether results are shown. The QUESTIONS are not part of it: read them with
`get_exam` and write them with `set_exam_questions`, which replaces the whole
list. Omit a question's `id` on first write and a stable id is minted; send an id
you read from `get_exam` to edit that question in place, because a learner's
in-flight attempt keys on it.

## Publishing is explicit, and it is a revision

Nothing publishes as a side effect. Run `course_health` first: `warnings` and
`issues` are advisory, and `blockers` is the only list that predicts a failed
publish. Then call `publish_course` deliberately — if it finds structural issues
it refuses once and names them; re-call with `confirm_issues: true` to publish
anyway. It returns the revision number, the live learner URL and the admin review
URL. `preview_course` gives you a single-use link into the published revision as
the reserved preview member.

Published courses are immutable revisions. Learners keep reading the previously
published revision until a new one lands, so a half-finished draft edit is never
visible to anyone.

Every write is audit-logged with `actorType: "mcp"` and syncs to the connected CRM
through the standard outbox — an API write and a UI edit are indistinguishable
downstream.
